 "post": {
  "title": "Say the Colour, Not the Word #shorts",
  "description": "Otto says no test can trick him, then Lila holds up a card. In 1935 John Ridley Stroop asked 100 college students to name the ink colour of colour words printed in a clashing colour. It took them about 74% longer than naming plain colour patches, because reading is so automatic you can't switch it off. Psychologists call it the Stroop effect. #psychology #shorts #brain #quiz",
